# wiringcliff -- WHAT PASS/FAIL CONTRACT DOES EACH OF FOUR MEASUREMENT HARNESSES ALREADY SUPPORT?

This is an ANALYSIS request about four existing files. Please do not write, propose or sketch any
code, any patch, or any guest instruction sequence -- I am asking what is ALREADY COMPUTED in each
file and what it would license as an assertion. Prose only.

## THE TREE
`C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\GXEMUL-SEC\` is the tree under review. Ignore any other `src/` or
`regress/` you find under `C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\` -- there are pristine-2021 upstream copies
there that will give you line numbers off by hundreds.

## SCOPE -- read EXACTLY these four files and nothing else

    regress/wdc_altoffset_probe.py
    regress/wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py
    regress/wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py
    regress/wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py

## SETTLED PREMISES -- derived here today, do not re-derive, but DO challenge if you find them wrong

1. `tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py` holds SIX exemptions dated 2026-09-20. Its comparison is
   `d < today`, so they stay valid ON 2026-09-20 and first red on 2026-09-21.
2. Of those six, two (`fbpending_drain_probe.py`, `hpcmips_ctor_probe.py`) end in `sys.exit()` with
   a real nonzero path. **The four files in scope above contain NO `sys.exit(` at all** -- they end
   in a bare `main()` call or simply fall off the end after writing a table file, so the process
   exits 0 whatever they measured. Each prints its own completion sentinel (`ALTOFF_SIG_DONE`,
   `ALTOFF_4BIT_DONE`, `ADDTOINBUF_RUNG3_DONE`).
3. Therefore wiring these four into a gate AS THEY STAND would ship four gate sections that cannot
   fail. In this project that is a named defect class -- "a cheap substitute for a real test, which
   returns the same answer whether or not the thing it checks is true" -- so the job is NOT wiring.

**Please check premise 2 rather than accepting it.** If any of the four does have a route to a
nonzero parent-process exit status that I have missed, say so first and plainly -- that is the most
useful answer you could give. Note `os._exit(127)` inside a forked pty child does NOT give the
parent a failure status, and a comment quoting a removed `exit(1)` is not a call.

## THE QUESTIONS -- answer per file, in this order

**Q1. WHAT DOES IT ALREADY DECIDE?** For each file, enumerate the values it computes that have a
KNOWN-CORRECT expectation somewhere in the file -- an expected constant, a comparison against a
control, a "want" written in a row, a documented reset value. I want the list of quantities on
which a pass/fail judgement is ALREADY available from what the file computes, distinguished from
quantities it merely prints. Quote the variable or row name for each.

**Q2. WHAT IS MERELY MEASURED?** The complement of Q1: which printed quantities have NO expectation
anywhere in the file, so that no assertion could be made about them without inventing one? These
are the ones where "wire it up" would require a new oracle rather than an exit status. Say which,
and say what the missing expectation would have to be a claim ABOUT.

**Q3. IS THERE A CONTROL ROW?** For each file, does it carry a LIVENESS control (a known value
returning through the same path, proving the instrument reached the device) and a DEVICE-SIGNATURE
control (a value that could only have come from the device, not from RAM or unmapped space)? Name
them, or say plainly that one or both is absent. A file with no liveness control cannot distinguish
"the property holds" from "nothing ran".

**Q4. OVERLAP.** Do any two of the four measure the same property? If two files' assertable sets
(Q1) are a subset/superset pair, that bears on whether all four need separate homes. State the
relationship extensionally -- which quantity in which file -- not as a summary judgement.

**Q5. THE HONEST RANKING.** Given only what you read: which of the four is CLOSEST to being able to
state a pass/fail contract from what it already computes, and which is FURTHEST? Give the reason in
terms of the specific quantities, not effort estimates.

Please do not simply ratify this brief's framing. If the right description of a file is "this was
never meant to be a detector and should be recorded as an exploratory measurement instead", say
that -- it is a legitimate answer and it changes the disposition rather than the schedule.

